stage_diagnostics.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "Congress should mandate data siloing as a risk-stratified architectural constraint on frontier AI developers, paired with independent third-party auditing, because the advisory and contractual privacy controls regulators have relied on for a decade have empirically collapsed under the velocity of AI data ingestion. The strongest version of the accelerationist counter is real: unified data access produces more capable agents, and capability gains translate into medical, scientific, and economic value that siloing will partially forfeit. That tradeoff is genuine, and any honest safetyist must concede it. But the choice is not between unified intelligence and siloed paralysis — it is between architectural containment scaled to hazard and a status quo in which boilerplate privacy waivers, system-prompt instructions, and FTC consent decrees demonstrably fail to constrain how training pipelines ingest, recombine, and memorize personal data.\n\nThe governing standard here is structural, not rhetorical: when a control mechanism operates at a layer the regulated system can bypass, that mechanism is advisory, not architectural. Prompt-level privacy instructions get jailbroken in published red-team evaluations; clickwrap consent fails the Restatement's basic test of meaningful assent; the FTC's 2023 Rite Aid order and the 2024 Avast settlement both document harms that occurred despite existing policy controls. Apply that standard to AI data flows and the conclusion follows directly — only constraints embedded at the data-architecture layer, where the model cannot route around them, will hold under the ingestion velocities frontier labs now operate at. Siloing is the architectural primitive that makes downstream privacy commitments verifiable rather than aspirational.\n\nSiloing alone, however, becomes compliance theater without verification infrastructure, which is why the operative variable is the audit regime, not the mandate itself. The model Congress should borrow is the tiered architecture already proven in adjacent domains: PCAOB-style independent audits for financial reporting, FDA Phase III oversight for clinical trials, and the NRC's graded inspection regime for nuclear facilities. Each scales scrutiny to hazard. Applied to AI, that means frontier developers training models above a defined compute or capability threshold — the same threshold logic Executive Order 14110 used before its rescission, and which the EU AI Act preserves for general-purpose models with systemic risk — face forensic-grade audit access and standardized transparency reporting, while lower-risk applications retain integration. Dynamic privacy-leakage benchmarking, the kind NIST is already piloting, gives auditors the empirical instrumentation to verify that siloing actually holds under adversarial probing. Without that scaffolding, any mandate degrades into paperwork.\n\nThe coalition case for siloing is that it addresses both the present documented harm — corporate surveillance harvesting that skeptics rightly foreground — and the structural risk of capability concentration that follows from unrestricted data aggregation. A worker whose keystroke data is scraped into a foundation model today and a public that faces an unrecoverable concentration of inference power tomorrow are harmed by the same underlying mechanism: the absence of architectural friction on data flows. Siloing forces provenance documentation, which directly addresses the training-data opacity problem the Copyright Office flagged in its July 2024 report. This is not a slippery-slope argument from speculative catastrophe; it is an incentive-design argument grounded in the observation that contractual remedies have not aligned developer behavior with stated user preferences, and architectural remedies might.\n\nTwo assumptions carry this position and deserve explicit disclosure. First, I assume architectural constraints are meaningfully harder to bypass than policy-level ones 