{
  "statement": "Mandatory data siloing targets the wrong architectural layer, and Congress should not codify a privacy theater that taxes capability while leaving incumbent surveillance untouched. Safetyist makes the strongest version of the opposing case: contractual and prompt-level controls have empirically failed, the FTC's actions against Rite Aid and the 2023 OpenAI consent decree show that promises don't bind, and architectural constraints — unlike policies — cannot be quietly bypassed by a product manager under quarterly pressure. That diagnosis is correct. The remedy is wrong. Privacy is a behavioral property verifiable by adversarial probing of outputs, not a topological property of data pipelines, and the architectural fix belongs at the cryptographic and output layers — differential privacy, federated learning, embedding-space transformations, and dynamic leakage benchmarks — not at raw-data isolation enforced by pre-deployment auditors.\n\nSafetyist conceded the crux directly: if siloed pipelines leak comparably to integrated ones under adversarial probing, the case for mandatory siloing collapses and emphasis should shift to differential privacy and output-side controls. The empirical record already meets that threshold. Carlini and collaborators' membership-inference and extraction benchmarks (2021-2023) demonstrate that siloed training corpora leak through model outputs at rates indistinguishable from integrated pipelines once the model is queried adversarially — siloing the inputs does not silo the behavior. Meanwhile, Apple's deployment of differential privacy at the 2017 WWDC scale and Google's federated learning rollout on Gboard show that privacy-preserving compute architectures ship at consumer scale. The burden of proof has flipped: the question is no longer whether output-side methods can match siloing, but whether siloing offers any marginal privacy benefit over them that justifies the capability cost.\n\nThat capability cost is not theoretical — it is a monitorability tax, and it is regressive. Capability-threshold-triggered audit regimes modeled on PCAOB, FDA, or NRC analogues require forensic overhead — provenance documentation, pipeline attestation, third-party auditor retainers — that Microsoft, Google, and Anthropic can absorb as a rounding error and that a fifteen-person open-weight lab or a university research group cannot. The 2023 Executive Order 14110's compute thresholds already preview this dynamic; layering siloing mandates on top compounds it. The incumbents Safetyist worries about already hold the first-party data troves — Gmail, Search, iMessage, Windows telemetry — so siloing mandates don't shrink their surveillance footprint, they shrink everyone else's training substrate. The net effect is moat-deepening dressed as privacy protection, and it foreclosesthe decentralized verification community whose existence Safetyist's audit regime depends on.\n\nIf Congress wants threshold-triggered scrutiny, the precondition is anti-capture institutional design — not a footnote, a precondition. That means rotating board membership on five-year non-renewable terms, sunset clauses that force reauthorization every seven years (the PCAOB's twenty-three-year unbroken tenure is the cautionary tale), structural separation of the halt-deployment power from the licensing power so a single captured node cannot freeze a competitor, and disclosure-based scaffolding — mandatory output-leakage benchmarks published quarterly, model cards with adversarial-probe results — rather than pre-deployment forensic gatekeeping. Automated circuit breakers triggered by leakage benchmark failures address the first-instance-harm concern Safetyist will raise; they are not post-hoc, they are continuous. This is what regulation that survives capture looks like, and it delivers verifiable privacy without conscripting the architecture of intelligence itself.\n\nTwo assumptions carry this argument and deserve to be named. First, I 