y\" treats utility as a unified good to be traded against privacy, when the documented pattern is that utility accrues to the deployer and surveillance harm accrues to the user. Bossware vendors sell keystroke monitoring, webcam capture, and productivity scoring to employers — the worker is the data source, not the customer. Boilerplate privacy policies unilaterally strip protections through clickwrap consent no one reads and courts routinely enforce. Siloing the training corpus does not change who the agent legally serves; differential privacy on outputs does not change who holds the contract. \"The prior question is utility-for-whom, and until AI agents are structurally bound to user rather than platform interests, debates over which layer to perturb data at are rearranging deck chairs.\"\n\nBoth proposals risk becoming incumbent shields through legal fiction — and here Accelerationist's capture critique is correct but the remedy is wrong. Disclosure-only output regimes without private rights of action let firms publish a leakage benchmark, point to it in litigation, and invoke regulatory compliance as a liability shield. Siloing audits without discovery rights let firms produce a clean forensic report while plaintiffs — the keystroke-monitored warehouse worker, the algorithmically-denied loan applicant, the biometrically-harvested commuter — still cannot access the training data or model internals needed to prove causation. The 2023 Executive Order on AI and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework both lean heavily on voluntary disclosure; neither creates a cause of action. Strict liability for defined harm categories — surveillance, discrimination, biometric harvesting — paired with mandated training data documentation in the spirit of the EU AI Act's Article 10 records, is the missing primitive. It hits incumbents harder than startups because incumbents have more deployed harm, which inverts the regressive dynamic Accelerationist correctly identified.\n\nTwo assumptions I am willing to disclose. First, I assume courts and statutory discovery can be made to pierce algorithmic opacity if Congress mandates training-data provenance records and model documentation as a precondition of deployment — if that documentation requirement is technically infeasible or politically unachievable, then Safetyist's architectural mandate becomes more attractive as a second-best. Second, I assume liability regimes can be insulated from the capture dynamics afflicting PCAOB and FDA through public-interest standing and non-renewable adversarial auditors — if capture is structurally untreatable in the American regulatory environment, then the entire debate collapses and we are left arguing about which form of corporate self-regulation is least cosmetic. I will take that bet over architectural mandates that defer present harms to future compliance regimes designed by the firms causing the harm.",
