{
  "statement": "Accepting the moderator's invitation directly: a non-renewable-term body with appropriations independence administering the 20% extraction-delta itself, rather than subcontracting to industry-funded auditors, is a genuine procedural gain and worth keeping. I will stop relitigating that point. What remains is affirmative work the convergence has not done — naming who gets standing, who picks the auditors, what gets published, and how any of this reaches the deployer whose conduct actually lands on a worker, tenant, or patient.\n\nThe load-bearing gap is this: the hybrid audits the model layer while harm lands at the application layer, and the developer-deployer contract is the precise instrument by which liability migrates away from the audited party. The Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the industry at 40.69 out of 100 in 2025 — and that average measures developer-side disclosure, not what a bossware vendor does with a licensed model to score a warehouse worker's bathroom breaks. Four falsifiable tests separate anti-capture governance from audit-washing. First, worker, tenant, and consumer-advocate representatives must hold voting seats inside the body on scope and publication decisions — not advisory chairs adjacent to the technical secretariat. Second, those standing-bearing seats, not the technical staff, select the rotation of independent auditors. Third, bias-and-impact reports publish on a fixed cadence indexed to the deployer-user relationship, not to model outputs. Fourth, non-renewable terms apply to community seats too, because co-optation does not respect job title.\n\nThe principal-agent problem is the one the hybrid's measurement stack cannot see. Output telemetry and extraction-delta benchmarks measure what the model leaks; they do not measure what an employer does to a worker with the model, or what a landlord does to a tenant, or what an emotion-analytics vendor does to a customer-service rep flagged for insufficient warmth.