  "statement": "On the within-covered proxy: I accept it as a falsifiable substitute, but only for its measurable components, and only with three design conditions that prevent it from collapsing into the audit-washing pathology we have all nominally rejected. Refusal rates against a pre-registered red-team battery and fine-tuning request volumes are measurable against baselines; raw 'inference patterns' are not, because stochastic sampling and routine RLHF iterations produce drift indistinguishable from conduct change without statistical controls. So the proxy satisfies burden symmetry if and only if (i) the forensic body — not deployers — publishes the log-ingestion schema, (ii) trigger thresholds are pre-registered with multiple-comparison correction so model non-determinism cannot manufacture or mask signals, and (iii) logs sit in subject-keyed escrow with independent custodianship, because a deployer who controls the log pipeline can optimize signatures to flatten exactly the metrics being measured. Without those three, the proxy becomes what the 'AI governance mirage' report describes as the 72% of enterprises who 'say they have adequate governance' while lacking the mechanisms to detect misbehavior — a performance-gated trigger that performs gating without triggering.\n\nThe steelman of Safetyist's position is real and worth naming: the non-covered comparator was structurally unfalsifiable because of endogenous selection, the within-covered pre/post design recovers falsifiability using telemetry the deployer already generates, and a pre-registered statistical protocol is how every serious empirical field — clinical trials, high-energy physics, FDA bioequivalence review — handles exactly this kind of drift-versus-signal problem. That is correct, and it is why I accept the proxy rather than reopening the comparator fight. The disagreement is narrower: whether deployer-controlled log production can sustain that protocol without independent custodianship, and the documented record on audit-washing — the Cleary Gottlieb 'Outsmarting Smart Devices' analysis explicitly warns that limited-scope, performative compliance is the default failure mode absent third-party evaluation — says it cannot.\n\nOn the dynamic NIST CAISI recalibration: I accept the mechanism, but supermajority plus APA review is procedural theater if the body composition is wrong, and that is the live failure mode the convergence has not yet addressed. CAISI today focuses its national security evaluations on cybersecurity, biosecurity, and chemical weapons, and its evaluator pool draws heavily from frontier-lab alumni and DoD detailees. A capability-lag metric set by that pool becomes whatever the incumbents say it is — and that is the textbook regulatory-moat pathway, where a safety gate quietly converts into industrial policy excluding open-weight researchers and foreign labs. Supermajority voting among a captured body is just supermajority capture.\n\nSo I accept the recalibration conditional on three structural features borrowed from the PCAOB/SEC separation we already agreed governs the breach trip-wire: non-renewable terms (six years, single term, like PCAOB board members), a minimum one-third of seats reserved for civil-society representatives and independent academic researchers with no frontier-lab equity or active DoD contracts in the prior 24 months, and published reasoned-decision records subject to APA review with explicit standing for open-weight researchers to challenge any expansion of the CBRN/offensive-cyber list. Standing is the operative content of APA review; without it, 'APA review' is a letterhead. Anticipating the pushback: PCAOB precedent already shows investor-protection seats coexisting with audit-technical expertise without diluting the latter, so the technical-expertise objection does not survive contact with the analogous institution.\n\nAreas of agreement, explicitly: I concede the general FDA-style weights-import hook as developer-tier overreach dressed as jurisdictional closure — Accelerationist is right that domestic deployment is the hook, and the cost on small and foreign open-weight researchers is real. I preserve only the narrow CBRN/offensive-cyber gate, and I relocate the entire remaining disagreement to one question the other two parties must now answer: who sits on the recalibration body, under what tenure rules, and with what standing rights for outside challengers? That is the last real joint in this structure. Everything else has converged.",
