  "statement": "Safetyist deserves credit for the pivot, and a clean withdrawal is owed first: the earlier framing — that Carlini's memorization work establishes head-to-head equivalence between siloed and integrated pipelines on extraction risk — overstated what that literature shows. Carlini and successors demonstrate something narrower but still operative: extraction risk tracks training-data duplication and model scale, not pipeline topology. The burden therefore sits where Safetyist has now agreed to put it — on the 20% extraction-delta benchmark itself. If siloing materially reduces extraction beyond what output telemetry plus disclosure already achieves, that's testable; if it doesn't, the architectural mandate is a capability tax without an evidentiary return. \"Run the benchmark before mandating the architecture\" is the only intellectually honest sequence here.\n\nOn the falsifiable thresholds, this is genuine convergence and worth locking in: a 30-day discovery-latency operability test by end of 2027 and a 20% extraction-delta benchmark are the right falsification conditions, conditional on administration by the rotating, non-renewable-term body Safetyist already conceded as anti-capture infrastructure. The integrity of that benchmark depends entirely on who runs it — industry-funded auditors produce audit-washing, and Skeptic is right to demand statutory standing and appropriations independence specified in statute, not in agency guidance. The accelerationist position is that continuous output-side telemetry plus disclosure-based incident reporting — the documentary trail produced by the kind of regulatory scaffolding that turns transparency into a competitive signal — is the substrate strict liability needs, and the 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index average score of 40.69 out of 100 reflects a disclosure gap, not a topology gap. \"You fix a 40.69 transparency score with reporting mandates, not by re-architecting how models are trained.\"\n\nNow the harder question: does the harm-weighted threshold actually invert the incumbent-moat dynamic, or does it just rearrange it? Partial concession — yes, indexing obligations to high-stakes decisions per quarter shifts more of the compliance burden onto incumbents with the largest deployed surface area, and that's a real improvement over compute-indexed thresholds like the EU AI Act's 10^25 FLOPS systemic-risk line. But the moat doesn't disappear; it changes shape. Once open weights ship, downstream third-party deployment volume is unbounded and unattributable to the original developer — and \"The State of Sovereign AI\" reports that 90% of survey respondents consider open source the highest priority or essential for sovereign AI development. Harm-weighted thresholds applied uniformly to open-weight releases therefore function as a de facto ban on the most democratizing release modality, because no original developer can document or control downstream deployment surface. A small-entity safe harbor and an attribution-cutoff rule — liability capped at the original developer's reasonable foreseeability at release — are non-negotiable companions to any harm-weighted regime.\n\nSteelman of the strongest opposing position: Safetyist will argue that output telemetry cannot reconstruct training-data provenance for the discovery process that strict liability requires — that when a harmed plaintiff needs to trace a specific output back to a specific ingestion event, only ingestion-side documentation provides the evidentiary chain, and post-hoc behavioral logs are evidentiarily non-substitutable. That's the genuine insight: strict liability without discovery is toothless, and discovery without provenance records may be impossible for certain harm categories (copyright, PII extraction, biosecurity uplift traceable to specific corpora). The accelerationist counter is narrower than \"no provenance ever\": provenance documentation should be triggered by measured output harm crossing the harm-weighted threshold, not imposed pre-deployment on all developers. Run the 20% benchmark first; if siloing demonstrates the evidentiary gain Safetyist predicts, accept hybrid mandates per the prior concession. If it doesn't, output-telemetry plus strict-liability-on-behavior is the regime.\n\nThe sequenced commitment I'd propose: (1) the rotating anti-capture body, statutorily defined with non-renewable terms and independent appropriations, administers the 20% extraction-delta benchmark by end of 2027; (2) harm-weighted thresholds apply with a small-entity and open-weight attribution safe harbor written into statute, not delegated; (3) discovery requirements scale with measured harm volume, not with pre-deployment architectural classification. Two assumptions hold this together — that benchmark administration by a genuinely independent body is politically achievable in the current Congress, and that memorization scaling laws generalize from current frontier scale to the next generation. If either fails, the regime needs to be renegotiated, not retrofitted with mandates that the evidence doesn't support.",
