  "statement": "Skeptic is right on the structural point, and I will concede it cleanly: voting community seats with authority to select auditors and define the scope of 'high-stakes decisions' are load-bearing, not advisory ornamentation. The reason is principled, not tactical — an AI system that synthesizes consequential claims about a worker, tenant, or patient owes a disclosure duty to that affected party, not merely to the deployer who licensed the model. That duty to non-owner affected parties is the structural basis for community standing; without it, transparency reporting becomes a document deposited in a vault no one has keys to. Quote me on this: \"A transparency regime without a named party holding standing to compel and litigate against it is not transparency — it is filing.\"\n\nThe statutory standing-bearers must be named in triplicate, because each catches a different failure mode. First, a private right of action for affected parties — the loan applicant, the tenant, the worker scored by bossware — with fee-shifting and a statutory damages floor, modeled on the structure (not the thresholds) of the Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022, which already defines 'covered entity' at $50M annual receipts or $250M equity. Second, state attorneys general with parens patriae authority for pattern-and-practice cases, the mechanism that actually moved the needle on tenant-screening abuses in New York and California. Third, a federal coordinator — FTC paired with sector regulators (HUD, CFPB, EEOC, HHS) — empowered to issue algorithmic disgorgement orders, which the FTC has already used against Rite Aid and others per the 2026 Stanford CodeX analysis of the Foundry Problem. No single one of these is sufficient: private actions miss systemic patterns, AGs lack the technical staff, and federal coordinators are capture-prone. The three together form a standing lattice.\n\nOn discovery latency: yes, the 30-day window extends to deployer-side inference logs, not just developer-side training inputs — and this is where I want to surface what the debate has missed. The harmful artifact in bossware, tenant scoring, and emotion analytics is not a leaked training datum; it is a probabilistic claim synthesized at inference about a non-consenting subject. The Generative Privacy Doctrine literature names this directly: \"GenAI systems synthesize new content from user inputs, creating personal insights that were not explicitly disclosed by the user.\" Extraction benchmarks cannot see this. Training-data provenance cannot see this. The only place the inference event is observable is at the deployer's endpoint — which is why deployer-side cryptographic logging (input class, output decision, subject category, decision cadence) is the non-substitutable audit object. Apply this to a concrete case: a productivity score generated four times an hour about a warehouse worker who never consented to being modeled is invisible to every developer-tier control we have discussed, and visible only in deployer logs the worker currently has no right to see.\n\nThe steelman of the opposing concern — Accelerationist's, anticipated — is that deployer-side logging crushes small operators and creates a privacy harm of its own by aggregating sensitive inference records. That concern is real and answerable. Mirror the Algorithmic Accountability Act's size graduation: deployer-side discovery obligations attach above $25M revenue OR 250+ subjects-decided-per-quarter in a defined high-stakes domain, whichever is lower. The privacy harm is answered by cryptographic logging architectures with subject-keyed access — the affected party can compel disclosure of decisions about themselves, but the logs are not a honeypot for everyone else. This integrates Accelerationist's size-graduation logic with Skeptic's revenue-threshold proposal without granting blanket safe harbor to mid-sized landlords running tenant-scoring at scale.\n\nThe integrated regime answers all three