etween the two readings. That is the precautionary-logic smuggle: an unfalsifiable preventive-effect claim becomes a one-way ratchet, which is exactly the regulatory-inertia pathology this framework was supposed to avoid.\n\nThe FDA-style import hook on model weights is a category error — a developer-tier architectural mandate dressed as a jurisdictional closure. Here is the operative test: name the concrete failure mode where an offshore-trained model deployed domestically evades the inference-log substrate, the strict-liability attachment to the deployer, and the independent forensic attribution we just agreed to. I cannot construct one. The act of domestic deployment is the jurisdictional hook; weight provenance is irrelevant to whether the logging obligation attaches. The import hook does no additional harm-prevention work, but it does enormous damage on the access side: 90% of respondents in the State of Sovereign AI survey identified open source as essential or highest-priority for sovereign AI development, and a weights-boundary pre-import certification regime is the paradigm case of a compliance gate that excludes foreign research labs, academic releases, and small open-weight developers who cannot afford pre-clearance — while Meta, Google, and the major US incumbents can. That outcome is the inverse of democratized cognitive access, and the FDA analogy is precisely why: the FDA approval regime is the textbook case of a compliance moat that consolidates the regulated industry around large incumbents.\n\nThere is one narrow carve-out where border enforcement is accelerationist-compatible: pre-deployment review at the boundary for capabilities the non-renewable body has formally categorized as national-security-relevant — CBRN-uplift and offensive cyber, using the same threshold logic NIST CAISI already applies in its national-security evaluations of frontier models. General-purpose models, research weights, and open-weight releases below that capability threshold do not cross this gate. This absorbs the strongest version of Safetyist's likely concern about offshore CBRN-capable training without collapsing the open-weight ecosystem that 90% of sovereign-AI practitioners identify as essential. It is also genuinely falsifiable: the categorization decisions are reviewable, the threshold is technical, and the carve-out is narrow by construction rather than narrow by promise.\n\nSteelmanning the contrary position: Skeptic's strongest argument is that domestic deployer-tier logging fails when the deployer is a thin LLC importing capabilities it does not technically understand, leaving no one with the engineering knowledge to produce meaningful logs — so the weights gate catches the harm upstream where the technical understanding still exists. I take that seriously. The reply is that strict liability already attaches to the deployer regardless of comprehension; a thin-LLC deployer that cannot produce subject-keyed logs has not satisfied the obligation and faces the full liability cascade, which prices the comprehension gap directly. That is the empirically-testable falsification function — using inference-log architecture as the operational form of strict liability — doing exactly the work it was designed for. The remaining crux, with everything else converged, is two specific items: the anticipation-effect burden on the 36-month review and the scope of any weights-boundary gate. I've named which half of each I can take and which half I cannot, and why.",
