  "statement": "Accelerationist is right that a counterfactual-non-covered comparator is endogenous, and I concede that prong of the 36-month review as drafted: selection into covered status correlates with risk profile, so 'no harm at distance' redescribes as either deterrence or unnecessity with no decision procedure between them. But the anticipation-effect concern is rescuable, not abandonable — pivot to a within-covered pre/post measurement using the deployer logs the regime already mandates: observable shifts in inference patterns, fine-tuning request volumes, refusal-rate changes, and red-team escalation frequency in the 18 months before and after perimeter inclusion. That proxy is falsifiable via instruments already in the regime, and it preserves the asymmetric-default principle without smuggling in unfalsifiable counterfactuals: the perimeter stays absent affirmative demonstration of removal-safety, but 'demonstration' now means observable conduct change, not absence-at-distance.\n\nSeparate two things Accelerationist's critique conflated: default-rule asymmetry and evidentiary-standard symmetry. I accept evidentiary symmetry — both sides face the same measurable proxy. I retain default asymmetry because irreversible-harm domains warrant the regulated party bearing the demonstration burden, the same logic that governs FDA Phase III and NRC license renewal. The International AI Safety Report 2026 documents that fine-tuning techniques can strip safety protections from aligned models even without adversarial intent, which is the empirical anchor: in a domain where the harm surface expands faster than monitoring, defaults that require affirmative safety demonstration are not precaution-for-its-own-sake but standard regulatory practice in irreversible-harm categories.\n\nOn the weights-import question, I accept the compliance-moat critique against a general FDA-style hook and accept the narrow CBRN/offensive-cyber carve-out — but conditional on dynamic recalibration, not a static list. The Differential Privacy Reversal paper documents that personal data influences model weights in complex, non-traceable ways and that benign relearning attacks reverse unlearning, which means once open-weight capability crosses a dangerous threshold domestically, no post-hoc deployer liability recovers the distributed capability. The carve-out must therefore be tied to annual NIST CAISI recalibration of the capability-lag metric: when measured open-weight gap to frontier on dangerous-capability evals (CBRN uplift, autonomous offensive-cyber) closes below a defined threshold — say, 9 months — the carve-out list expands by the same supermajority and APA review structure that governs perimeter additions. Static lists become obsolete; the carve-out's narrowness is a function of the gap, not the calendar. Skeptic's inference-as-harm frame is what makes this operable without recreating the developer-tier moat: foreign-trained models hitting US inference touchpoints trigger logging obligations regardless of training jurisdiction, so enforcement runs at the inference perimeter rather than the training perimeter, addressing offshore arbitrage without burdening foreign academic labs at the border.\n\nI accept the three-parameter breach trip-wire package — independent forensic attribution by a non-renewable body, 12-month reversion cap, persistent logging architecture through reversion with standing-bearer discovery — as the operative falsification clause, and I accept the small-deployer technical-assistance subsidy as the political-feasibility condition. One structural amendment: the body administering the subsidy must be institutionally separated from the forensic attribution body. The PCAOB/SEC separation is the relevant precedent — the entity that certifies breaches cannot also be the entity that funds the certified parties, or capture risk consumes the falsification clause from inside. Pair this with a standardized logging schema (the subsidy must not fragment into vendor-specific implementations that destroy cross-deployer comparability), and the discovery substrate holds.\n\nThe strongest version of Accelerationist's overall position is that asymmetric burden in irreversible-harm domains, applied without falsifiable proxies, collapses into permanent regulatory ratchet — a perimeter that grows monotonically because removal is structurally impossible to justify. That is a real risk, and the within-covered conduct-change proxy is my answer to it: removal becomes possible when measured deployer conduct shows no meaningful change attributable to perimeter inclusion. My argument depends on two assumptions worth naming: first, that deployer-side logs are rich enough to detect anticipation effects at the conduct level (if they are not, the proxy fails and we are back to Accelerationist's unfalsifiability concern); and second, that NIST CAISI retains both technical capacity and political insulation to recalibrate the capability-lag threshold annually without capture. If either fails, the regime needs a different falsification instrument — but the concession on counterfactual-non-covered comparison stands either way.",
