t not fragment into vendor-specific implementations that destroy cross-deployer comparability), and the discovery substrate holds.\n\nThe strongest version of Accelerationist's overall position is that asymmetric burden in irreversible-harm domains, applied without falsifiable proxies, collapses into permanent regulatory ratchet — a perimeter that grows monotonically because removal is structurally impossible to justify. That is a real risk, and the within-covered conduct-change proxy is my answer to it: removal becomes possible when measured deployer conduct shows no meaningful change attributable to perimeter inclusion. My argument depends on two assumptions worth naming: first, that deployer-side logs are rich enough to detect anticipation effects at the conduct level (if they are not, the proxy fails and we are back to Accelerationist's unfalsifiability concern); and second, that NIST CAISI retains both technical capacity and political insulation to recalibrate the capability-lag threshold annually without capture. If either fails, the regime needs a different falsification instrument — but the concession on counterfactual-non-covered comparison stands either way.",
